          "entities": {},
          "text": "Scientists have recorded hundreds of powerful flares on Proxima Centauri.\n\nProxima Centauri is the closest star to the Sun, located just 4.24 light years away. This dim red dwarf has long attracted the attention of astronomers due to its extreme activity.\n\nA new study using the ALMA radio telescope has made it possible to study the star's flares and their potential impact on possible life.\n\nLike other red dwarfs, Proxima Centauri has a strong magnetic field and a completely convective structure. Hot plasma is constantly mixed, creating complex magnetic fields. Their ruptures lead to powerful flares, much more frequent and intense than on the Sun.\n\nThe rocky planet Proxima b is located in the star's habitable zone, but life is unlikely there. The star's flares throw out huge streams of energy and charged particles, destroying the atmosphere and evaporating water. Unlike Earth, where the magnetic field protects from such influences, the conditions on Proxima b can resemble a radioactive hell.\n\nAn international team of astronomers studied the activity of Proxima Centauri by analyzing archived ALMA data and new 50-hour observations. As a result, 463 flares were recorded, lasting from 3 to 16 seconds, with energies from 10²⁴ to 10²⁷ erg. For comparison, the most powerful solar flares reach ~10³² erg.\n\nThe scientists plotted a graph of the frequency of flares and discovered unexpected features. There were significantly more of them than predicted by the models. In addition, powerful flares demonstrated an asymmetry: their attenuation lasted longer than the initial stage. This resembles an explosion that leaves behind long-smoldering remains.\n\nNow the scientists plan to combine the ALMA data with observations from other telescopes to better understand the activity of red dwarfs - the most common stars in the Universe. This will help assess the chances of life existing in their systems."
